{"name":"Wagon Train","hex":"#6d4d27","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wagon train","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A number of horse-drawn wagons traveling together for safety."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wagon_train"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wagon Train","blocks":[{"text":"Wagon Train is an American Western television series that aired for eight seasons, first on the NBC television network (1957–1962) and then on ABC (1962–1965). Wagon Train debuted on September 18, 1957, and reached the top of the Nielsen ratings. It is the fictional adventure story of a large westbound wagon train through the American frontier from Missouri to California. Its format attracted famous guest stars for each episode, appearing as travelers or residents of the settlements whom the regular cast encountered."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/a/aa/Wagon_Train_.jpg/330px-Wagon_Train_.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wagon_Train"},{"title":"Wagon train","blocks":[{"text":"A wagon train is a group of wagons traveling together. Before the extensive use of military vehicles, baggage trains followed an army with supplies and ammunition."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wagon_train"},{"title":"Wagon","blocks":[{"text":"A wagon is a heavy four-wheeled vehicle pulled by draft animals or on occasion by humans, used for transporting goods, commodities, agricultural materials, supplies and sometimes people."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Wagon_Ride_P9190071.jpg/330px-Wagon_Ride_P9190071.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wagon"},{"title":"Train","blocks":[{"text":"A train is a series of connected vehicles that run along a railway track and transport people or freight. Trains are typically pulled or pushed by locomotives, though some are self-propelled, such as multiple units or railcars. Passengers and cargo are carried in railroad cars, also known as wagons or carriages. Trains are designed to a certain gauge, or distance between rails. Most trains operate on steel tracks with steel wheels, the low friction of which makes them more efficient than other forms of transport. Many countries use rail transport."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/%D0%9F%D0%BE%D0%B5%D0%B7%D0%B4_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D1%84%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B5_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D1%8B_%D0%A8%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B8%D1%89%D0%B5._%D0%92%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B5%D0%B6%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%BB%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%82%D1%8C.jpg/330px-%D0%9F%D0%BE%D0%B5%D0%B7%D0%B4_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D1%84%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B5_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D1%8B_%D0%A8%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B8%D1%89%D0%B5._%D0%92%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B5%D0%B6%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%BB%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%82%D1%8C.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Train"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Robert_Horton_Ward_Bond_Wagon_Train.JPG/500px-Robert_Horton_Ward_Bond_Wagon_Train.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Robert_Horton_Ward_Bond_Wagon_Train.JPG/500px-Robert_Horton_Ward_Bond_Wagon_Train.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Robert Horton and Ward Bond","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARobert_Horton_Ward_Bond_Wagon_Train.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Robert_Horton_Wagon_Train.JPG/500px-Robert_Horton_Wagon_Train.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Robert_Horton_Wagon_Train.JPG/500px-Robert_Horton_Wagon_Trai